      "content": "To derive the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle rigorously, we examine a normalized wave function $\\psi(x)$ and its Fourier transform $\\phi(k) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int \\psi(x) e^{-ikx} dx$, which represents the momentum distribution in quantum mechanics. By defining the uncertainties $\\Delta x = \\sqrt{\\langle x^2 \\rangle - \\langle x \\rangle^2}$ and $\\Delta p = \\hbar \\sqrt{\\langle k^2 \\rangle - \\langle k \\rangle^2}$, we utilize Cauchy-Schwarz inequality properties on the vector space formed by the state $\\psi$ to show that $(\\Delta A)^2 (\\Delta B)^2 \\geq |\\frac{1}{2i} \\langle [\\hat{A}, \\hat{B}] \\rangle|^2$. Taking $\\hat{A} = \\hat{x}$ and $\\hat{B} = \\hat{p}$ yields the rigorous bound $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2$, proving that the product of standard deviations cannot vanish because position and momentum operators do not share a common set of eigenvectors.\nThe deep",
